KNOWNBUG libcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11 --stdlib libc++
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
too many addressed objects
no body for callee
--
libc++ defines std::function's members outside the class, in the partial
specialization function<_Rp(_ArgTypes...)>.  For std::function<int()> the
converting constructor template `function(_Fp)' still has no body, so the
call returns a nondeterministic value (not reduced; the hand-written shape
works).  Its call operator was fixed by
cpp11_partial_specialization_member_defined_outside_parameter_pack, and
dropping the virtual tables that no reachable code uses removed the 400
candidate targets of `__f_->destroy()' that exceeded the default
--object-bits.  With libstdc++ the program verifies in under a second.
